# Copy to config/<env>.env -- dev.env, stg.env, prod.env -- and start the API
# with ../run.sh. The files are committed, so a secret is never written into
# one: it is a reference to Vault, fetched when the file is loaded (see
# ../vault-env.sh). The field defaults to the variable's own name:
#
#   POSTGRES_PASSWORD=vault:secret/ziantsabit/prod/be
#   RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY=vault:secret/ziantsabit/prod/be#SOME_OTHER_FIELD
#
#
#   ./run.sh dev              ./gradlew bootRun on this machine
#   ./run.sh prod --docker    docker compose up --build in a container
#
# The app and the compose files use different names for some of the same
# settings: the app reads the first section's names, the compose files the
# COMPOSE section's at the bottom. Write the COMPOSE names and one file serves
# both modes: for a host run, run.sh maps RUSTFS_*, SITE_ORIGINS and
# MEDIA_ORIGIN onto the app's names and host.docker.internal onto localhost.

# Postgres is the only backend; there is no embedded fallback, and nothing here
# starts one -- point these at your own server.
POSTGRES_DB=zian_tsabit_be
POSTGRES_USER=zian_tsabit_be
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# TLS for the database connection. Unset means the driver's default, 'prefer',
# which silently falls back to plaintext when the server has no TLS -- fine for
# a server on the same machine, not fine across a network.
#   require      encrypt, but do not check who answered
#   verify-full  encrypt and authenticate the server; needs the CA below
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=
# Path *inside the container* to the CA that signed the server certificate, so
# the file has to be mounted in. Only read for verify-ca / verify-full.
# POSTGRES_SSLROOTCERT=

# Create this account on startup if it does not exist yet -- the replacement for
# `manage.py createsuperuser`. An existing account is never modified, and one
# carried over from the Django backend keeps its password.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Uploaded post images go to an S3-compatible bucket (your RustFS server). The
# bucket must already exist, allow anonymous s3:GetObject on its objects, and
# allow cross-origin GET/HEAD -- nothing in this repo creates it.
AWS_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME=ziantsabit-media
AWS_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=rustfsadmin
AWS_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=rustfsadmin
# API -> storage.
AWS_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:9000
# browser -> storage, i.e. what ends up in every image URL. Only differs from
# the line above when the API reaches the bucket by a name the browser cannot,
# which is exactly the case inside Docker.
# AWS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_URL=http://localhost:9000
# MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE=10485760

# Space-separated. DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS is still read as a fallback.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost 127.0.0.1 [::1]
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173
# Take the scheme and client address from X-Forwarded-* -- set to `native` only
# behind a proxy, since a client can send those headers too.
# FORWARD_HEADERS_STRATEGY=none

# What a *stranger* may write, per client address: leaving a comment, and
# tapping a reaction. "<number>/<second|minute|hour|day>". Counted in memory, so
# a restart resets them.
# COMMENT_RATE_LIMIT=20/hour
# REACTION_RATE_LIMIT=60/min


# COMPOSE
# -------
# Read by docker-compose.yml (Compose reads .env automatically) and interpolated
# into the `api` service. Compose refuses to start if a required one is missing.
#
#   docker compose up -d --build
#
# Inside the container `localhost` is the container itself: a server on the
# Docker host is `host.docker.internal`.

# Required.
# POSTGRES_HOST=host.docker.internal
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY=
# RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY=
# AWS_S3_ENDPOINT_URL=http://host.docker.internal:9000
# Optional, with these defaults.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# POSTGRES_DB=zian_tsabit_be
# POSTGRES_USER=zian_tsabit_be
# POSTGRES_SSLMODE=prefer          require / verify-full across a network
# POSTGRES_SSLROOTCERT=            only for verify-full; mount the CA in too
# AWS_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME=ziantsabit-media
# AWS_S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT_URL=      browser -> storage; defaults to the endpoint

# PRODUCTION
# ----------
# Additionally required by docker-compose.prod.yml:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up -d --build

# The API's own hostname, space-separated. Not the site's.
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=api.example.com
# The origin(s) serving the SPA. Fills both CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS and
# CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS.
# SITE_ORIGINS=https://example.com https://www.example.com
# browser -> object storage. Written into the stored URL of every upload, so
# settle on it before the first one: changing it later 404s every image already
# published.
# MEDIA_ORIGIN=https://media.example.com
# DJANGO_SECRET_KEY is no longer read: sessions are stored server-side and the
# CSRF token is random, so nothing is signed with it. It can stay in .env.
